#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697653 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t^2-t,\\qquad y=\\frac{t+1}{t-1},\\qquad -3\\le t\\le 0.\n$$\n\nFill in the three blanks in the table. Give exact values.\n\n| $t$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $12$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-2}} | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The missing $x$-entry is in the column $t=-2$. Substitute into $x=t^2-t$:\n\n$$\nx(-2)=(-2)^2-(-2)=4+2=6.\n$$\n\nFor the missing $y$-entries, substitute the column's parameter into both the numerator and the denominator of $y=\\frac{t+1}{t-1}$:\n\n$$\ny(-3)=\\frac{-3+1}{-3-1}=\\frac{-2}{-4}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\n$$\ny(0)=\\frac{0+1}{0-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe denominators are nonzero at all the listed parameter values. The completed table is\n\n| $t$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $12$ | $6$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $0$ | $-1$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697893 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t(t-2),\\qquad y=t(t+2),\\qquad -2\\leq t\\leq 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Create a table of paired coordinates. First calculate the $x$-values:\n\n| $t$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $8$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow calculate the $y$-values at the same parameter values:\n\n| $t$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $8$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $8$ |\n\nThe successive points are $(8,0)$, $(3,-1)$, $(0,0)$, $(-1,3)$ and $(0,8)$. Both coordinates turn within the interval: $x=(t-1)^2-1$ has minimum $-1$ at $t=1$, while $y=(t+1)^2-1$ has minimum $-1$ at $t=-1$. Plot a smooth curve through the paired points in parameter order. It dips below the $x$-axis through $(3,-1)$, passes through the origin, then bends left of the $y$-axis through $(-1,3)$ before reaching $(0,8)$. All five choices share the endpoints and the origin, so those three checkpoints alone cannot decide the answer. Check both interior bends.\n\n![The curve through the calculated points](images/86/8644be497d26e662b3b867539ee7b8d0dd812fac915e55db7b05045592a4fc6e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697894 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t+2,\\qquad y=1-t,\\qquad -2\\leq t\\leq 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Create a table of paired coordinates. First calculate the $x$-values:\n\n| $t$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow calculate the $y$-values at the same parameter values:\n\n| $t$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe paired coordinates are $(0,3)$, $(1,2)$, $(2,1)$, $(3,0)$ and $(4,-1)$. They lie on a decreasing straight line. Keep only the segment from $(0,3)$ to $(4,-1)$, with both endpoints included. A line with the right slope but different endpoints is not the required graph.\n\n![The curve through the calculated points](images/82/8227fb9f808f8b7622b2d3910efa11ce6d87b8085b4d7e24a77a342306532ee4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697897 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t^2-1,\\qquad y=2-t,\\qquad -1\\leq t\\leq 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Create a table of paired coordinates. First calculate the $x$-values:\n\n| $t$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow calculate the $y$-values at the same parameter values:\n\n| $t$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nThe points are $(0,3)$, $(-1,2)$, $(0,1)$ and $(3,0)$. As $t$ runs from $-1$ to $0$, $x$ decreases to $-1$; as $t$ runs from $0$ to $2$, $x$ increases to $3$. Meanwhile $y$ decreases throughout. Plot the smooth right-opening arc through these points. Its leftmost point is $(-1,2)$, and its included endpoints are $(0,3)$ and $(3,0)$. The two arms have different lengths because the parameter interval is asymmetric.\n\n![The curve through the calculated points](images/93/9346e80ef10f528dd956f6d11b511b106e39c9d2724a6be0c6e60f9e73afe183.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697915 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2+2t-3,\\quad y=4t-t^2,\\quad t>0,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$." :question/worked-solution "Complete the square for each coordinate, keeping the restriction $t>0$ throughout.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=t^2+2t-3 \\\\\n  &=(t+1)^2-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unrestricted minimum $-4$ would require $t=-1$, which is not allowed. In the given domain, $t+1>1$, so $(t+1)^2>1$ and $x>-3$. The boundary value $-3$ would require $t=0$ or $t=-2$, neither of which is allowed. Every value $x>-3$ is attained by $t=-1+\\sqrt{x+4}>0$. Thus the range of $x$ is $x>-3$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &=4t-t^2 \\\\\n  &=-(t-2)^2+4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $y\\le 4$. The maximum $4$ is attained at $t=2$, which is allowed even though the parameter inequality is strict. Every value $y\\le 4$ is attained by $t=2+\\sqrt{4-y}>0$, so there is no lower bound.\n\nTherefore, the coordinate ranges are $x>-3$ and $y\\le 4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697918 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2-4t+1,\\quad y=3-t^2,\\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$, complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=t^2-4t+1 \\\\\n  &=(t^2-4t+4)-3 \\\\\n  &=(t-2)^2-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square takes every nonnegative value as $t$ ranges over all real numbers. Thus $x\\ge -3$, with equality at $t=2$, and there is no upper bound.\n\nFor $y$, the fact that $t^2\\ge 0$ gives $y=3-t^2\\le 3$. Equality occurs at $t=0$, and $y$ takes every value below $3$.\n\nTherefore, the coordinate ranges are $x\\ge -3$ and $y\\le 3$. These bounds need not occur at the same parameter value; they describe the two coordinate ranges separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186697926 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4-2t,\\quad y=t^2+1,\\quad t\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $t\\ge 1$, multiplying by $-2$ reverses the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &\\ge 1 \\\\\n-2t &\\le -2 \\\\\n4-2t &\\le 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=2$ is attained at $t=1$. As $t$ increases without bound, $x$ decreases without bound. Every value below $2$ occurs: for any $x\\le 2$, the parameter $t=(4-x)/2$ satisfies $t\\ge 1$.\n\nTherefore, the range is $x\\le 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698030 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(9 - 5 x\\right)^{3}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=9 - 5 x$ and $F(g)=g^{3}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=3 g^{2}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(3 \\left(9 - 5 x\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-5\\right) \\\\ &=- 15 \\left(9 - 5 x\\right)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698031 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(9 x - 11\\right)^{6}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=9 x - 11$ and $F(g)=g^{6}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=6 g^{5}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(6 \\left(9 x - 11\\right)^{5}\\right)\\cdot\\left(9\\right) \\\\ &=54 \\left(9 x - 11\\right)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698040 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(1 - 5 x\\right)^{8}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1 - 5 x$ and $F(g)=g^{8}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=8 g^{7}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(8 \\left(1 - 5 x\\right)^{7}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-5\\right) \\\\ &=- 40 \\left(1 - 5 x\\right)^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698042 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(8 - 6 x\\right)^{5}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=8 - 6 x$ and $F(g)=g^{5}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=5 g^{4}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(5 \\left(8 - 6 x\\right)^{4}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-6\\right) \\\\ &=- 30 \\left(8 - 6 x\\right)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698050 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\left(- 2 x - 2\\right)^{8}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=- 2 x - 2$ and $F(g)=g^{8}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} F^{\\prime}(g)&=8 g^{7}, \\\\ g^{\\prime}(x)&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f^{\\prime}(x)&=F^{\\prime}(g(x))\\cdot g^{\\prime}(x) \\\\ &=\\left(8 \\left(- 2 x - 2\\right)^{7}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &=- 16 \\left(- 2 x - 2\\right)^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698065 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2-3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta-4,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x\\le$ {{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}} $\\le y$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately. On $0\\le\\theta<2\\pi$, both sine and cosine attain every value from $-1$ to $1$.\n\nFor $x$, multiplying by $-3$ reverses the inequalities. Add $2$, then write the bounds in increasing order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta \\le 1 \\\\\n3 &\\ge -3\\cos\\theta \\ge -3 \\\\\n5 &\\ge 2-3\\cos\\theta \\ge -1 \\\\\n-1 &\\le x\\le 5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y$, multiply by $2$ and subtract $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta \\le 1 \\\\\n-2 &\\le 2\\sin\\theta \\le 2 \\\\\n-6 &\\le 2\\sin\\theta-4 \\le -2 \\\\\n-6 &\\le y\\le -2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth endpoints of each range are attained. Therefore, $-1\\le x\\le 5$ and $-6\\le y\\le -2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698070 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta-1,\\qquad 0<\\theta\\le\\frac{3\\pi}{2},\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$? Use $<$ or $\\le$ as appropriate in each comparison blank.\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x\\le$ {{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}} $\\le y$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "The parameter interval does not cover a full turn. Check the sine and cosine extrema within the interval, as well as the excluded and included endpoints.\n\nFor $x$, cosine reaches $-1$ at $\\theta=\\pi$. It approaches $1$ as $\\theta$ approaches $0$ from above, but never equals $1$ on the given interval. Thus $-1\\le\\cos\\theta<1$. Multiplying by $-2$ reverses the comparisons, including the strict one:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta <1 \\\\\n2 &\\ge -2\\cos\\theta >-2 \\\\\n3 &\\ge 1-2\\cos\\theta >-1 \\\\\n-1 &<x\\le 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe upper bound $3$ occurs at $\\theta=\\pi$; the lower bound $-1$ is not attained.\n\nFor $y$, sine reaches $1$ at the interior point $\\theta=\\pi/2$ and $-1$ at the included endpoint $\\theta=3\\pi/2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta \\le 1 \\\\\n-3 &\\le 3\\sin\\theta \\le 3 \\\\\n-4 &\\le 3\\sin\\theta-1 \\le 2 \\\\\n-4 &\\le y\\le 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ranges are $-1<x\\le 3$ and $-4\\le y\\le 2$. Sine attains both extremes even though $\\theta=0$ is excluded. Continuity ensures that all values between the stated bounds occur. The coordinate ranges are considered separately; their extremes need not occur at the same parameter value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698072 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\le\\theta<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately. On $0\\le\\theta<2\\pi$, both sine and cosine attain every value from $-1$ to $1$.\n\nFor $x$, multiply the cosine bounds by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta \\le 1 \\\\\n-3 &\\le 3\\cos\\theta \\le 3 \\\\\n-3 &\\le x \\le 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y$, multiply the sine bounds by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta \\le 1 \\\\\n-5 &\\le 5\\sin\\theta \\le 5 \\\\\n-5 &\\le y \\le 5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ranges are $-3\\le x\\le 3$ and $-5\\le y\\le 5$. All four bounds are attained within the parameter interval; excluding $2\\pi$ does not remove any coordinate bound."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698121 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=- 2 t - 1,\\qquad y=- t^{2} - 1,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the three blanks in the table. Give exact values.\n\nUse the value of $t$ at the top of each column in the formula for that row. For example, $x(-2)=- 2 \\left(-2\\right) - 1=3$.\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $3$ | {{field-2}} | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-5$ |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | {{field-1}} | $-2$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "The missing entry is in the $y$-row under $t=0$. Substitute into $y=- t^{2} - 1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) &=- \\left(0\\right)^{2} - 1 \\\\ &=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $x$-row under $t=-1$. Substitute into $x=- 2 t - 1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-1) &=- 2 \\left(-1\\right) - 1 \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $y$-row under $t=2$. Substitute into $y=- t^{2} - 1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) &=- \\left(2\\right)^{2} - 1 \\\\ &=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-5$ |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698122 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=1 - t,\\qquad y=2 - t^{2},\\qquad -2\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the three blanks in the table. Give exact values.\n\nUse the value of $t$ at the top of each column in the formula for that row. For example, $x(-2)=1 - \\left(-2\\right)=3$.\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $3$ | {{field-2}} | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $1$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-1}} | $-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The missing entry is in the $y$-row under $t=1$. Substitute into $y=2 - t^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) &=2 - \\left(1\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $x$-row under $t=-1$. Substitute into $x=1 - t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-1) &=1 - \\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $y$-row under $t=0$. Substitute into $y=2 - t^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) &=2 - \\left(0\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698133 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t^{2} - 2,\\qquad y=3 t + 3,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the three blanks in the table. Give exact values.\n\nUse the value of $t$ at the top of each column in the formula for that row. For example, $x(-2)=\\left(-2\\right)^{2} - 2=2$.\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $-2$ | $-1$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |" :question/worked-solution "The missing entry is in the $x$-row under $t=-1$. Substitute into $x=t^{2} - 2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-1) &=\\left(-1\\right)^{2} - 2 \\\\ &=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $y$-row under $t=-2$. Substitute into $y=3 t + 3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2) &=3 \\left(-2\\right) + 3 \\\\ &=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $x$-row under $t=2$. Substitute into $x=t^{2} - 2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) &=\\left(2\\right)^{2} - 2 \\\\ &=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698140 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} - 2,\\qquad y=2 t - 3,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the three blanks in the table. Give exact values.\n\nUse the value of $t$ at the top of each column in the formula for that row. For example, $x(-2)=- \\left(-2\\right)^{2} - 2=-6$.\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | {{field-1}} | $-2$ | $-3$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | {{field-2}} | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |" :question/worked-solution "The missing entry is in the $x$-row under $t=-1$. Substitute into $x=- t^{2} - 2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-1) &=- \\left(-1\\right)^{2} - 2 \\\\ &=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $y$-row under $t=-2$. Substitute into $y=2 t - 3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2) &=2 \\left(-2\\right) - 3 \\\\ &=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $x$-row under $t=2$. Substitute into $x=- t^{2} - 2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) &=- \\left(2\\right)^{2} - 2 \\\\ &=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698141 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3 t - 1,\\qquad y=- t^{2} - 2,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the three blanks in the table. Give exact values.\n\nUse the value of $t$ at the top of each column in the formula for that row. For example, $x(-1)=3 \\left(-1\\right) - 1=-4$.\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-3}} | $-4$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "The missing entry is in the $y$-row under $t=0$. Substitute into $y=- t^{2} - 2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) &=- \\left(0\\right)^{2} - 2 \\\\ &=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $y$-row under $t=2$. Substitute into $y=- t^{2} - 2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) &=- \\left(2\\right)^{2} - 2 \\\\ &=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing entry is in the $x$-row under $t=-2$. Substitute into $x=3 t - 1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-2) &=3 \\left(-2\\right) - 1 \\\\ &=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe completed table is\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698167 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t - 1,\\quad y=2 t - 1,\\quad t\\ge -1 ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=t - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine coefficient is $1$. The boundary $t=-1$ gives $x=-2$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded above, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\\ge -2$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &=2 t - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine coefficient is $2$. The boundary $t=-1$ gives $y=-3$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded above, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\\ge -3$.\n\nTherefore, $x\\ge -2,\\,y\\ge -3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698175 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4 - t^{2},\\quad y=- t^{2} + 4 t - 8,\\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty) ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=4 - t^{2} \\\\ &=4 - t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square takes every nonnegative value. Equality occurs at $t=0$. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\\le 4$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &=- t^{2} + 4 t - 8 \\\\ &=- \\left(t - 2\\right)^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square takes every nonnegative value. Equality occurs at $t=2$. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\\le -4$.\n\nTherefore, $x\\le 4,\\,y\\le -4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698191 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{- t}+4,\\quad y=-2\\sqrt{3 - t},\\quad t\\le -1 ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$.\n\n$x$ {{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n\n$y$ {{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=-\\sqrt{- t}+4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radicand starts at $1$ and increases without bound along the allowed half-line. The square root is increasing, and its exterior coefficient is $-1$. The boundary $t=-1$ gives $x=3$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\\le 3$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &=-2\\sqrt{3 - t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radicand starts at $4$ and increases without bound along the allowed half-line. The square root is increasing, and its exterior coefficient is $-2$. The boundary $t=-1$ gives $y=-4$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\\le -4$.\n\nTherefore, $x\\le 3,\\,y\\le -4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698193 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^{2} + 3,\\quad y=- 2 t - 2,\\quad t\\ge -2 ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$.\n\n$x$ {{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n\n$y$ {{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=t^{2} + 3 \\\\ &=t^{2} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter $t=0$ is inside the domain, so its value $3$ is attained even if the parameter boundary is excluded. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded above, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\\ge 3$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &=- 2 t - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine coefficient is $-2$. The boundary $t=-2$ gives $y=2$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\\le 2$.\n\nTherefore, $x\\ge 3,\\,y\\le 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698198 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- 2 t - 4,\\quad y=4 - 2 t,\\quad t\\ge -1 ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y$." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=- 2 t - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine coefficient is $-2$. The boundary $t=-1$ gives $x=-2$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\\le -2$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &=4 - 2 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe affine coefficient is $-2$. The boundary $t=-1$ gives $y=6$ and is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\\le 6$.\n\nTherefore, $x\\le -2,\\,y\\le 6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698224 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nx=-4\\cos\\theta-2,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta-2,\\\\\n0\\le \\theta<2\\pi,\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x\\le$ {{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}} $\\le y$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\cos\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiplying by $-4$ reverses both comparisons; translate by $-2$, then write the bounds in increasing order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n4 &\\ge-4\\cos\\theta\\ge-4 \\\\\n2 &\\ge-4\\cos\\theta-2\\ge-6 \\\\\n-6 &\\le x\\le 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-6\\le x\\le 2$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\sin\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiply by $5$ and translate by $-2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n-5 &\\le 5\\sin\\theta\\le 5 \\\\\n-7 &\\le 5\\sin\\theta-2\\le 3 \\\\\n-7 &\\le y\\le 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-7\\le y\\le 3$.\n\nContinuity gives every value between the stated bounds. The coordinate extrema need not occur at the same parameter value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698228 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nx=4\\cos\\theta+3,\\qquad y=-2\\sin\\theta-3,\\\\\n0\\le \\theta<2\\pi,\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x\\le$ {{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}} $\\le y$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\cos\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiply by $4$ and translate by $3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n-4 &\\le 4\\cos\\theta\\le 4 \\\\\n-1 &\\le 4\\cos\\theta+3\\le 7 \\\\\n-1 &\\le x\\le 7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-1\\le x\\le 7$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\sin\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiplying by $-2$ reverses both comparisons; translate by $-3$, then write the bounds in increasing order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n2 &\\ge-2\\sin\\theta\\ge-2 \\\\\n-1 &\\ge-2\\sin\\theta-3\\ge-5 \\\\\n-5 &\\le y\\le -1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-5\\le y\\le -1$.\n\nContinuity gives every value between the stated bounds. The coordinate extrema need not occur at the same parameter value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698230 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nx=4\\cos\\theta+1,\\qquad y=4\\sin\\theta-3,\\\\\n0\\le \\theta<2\\pi,\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x\\le$ {{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}} $\\le y$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\cos\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiply by $4$ and translate by $1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n-4 &\\le 4\\cos\\theta\\le 4 \\\\\n-3 &\\le 4\\cos\\theta+1\\le 5 \\\\\n-3 &\\le x\\le 5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-3\\le x\\le 5$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\sin\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiply by $4$ and translate by $-3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n-4 &\\le 4\\sin\\theta\\le 4 \\\\\n-7 &\\le 4\\sin\\theta-3\\le 1 \\\\\n-7 &\\le y\\le 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-7\\le y\\le 1$.\n\nContinuity gives every value between the stated bounds. The coordinate extrema need not occur at the same parameter value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698242 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nx=-2\\sin\\theta-3,\\qquad y=2\\cos\\theta-2,\\\\\n0\\le \\theta<2\\pi,\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x\\le$ {{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}} $\\le y$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\sin\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiplying by $-2$ reverses both comparisons; translate by $-3$, then write the bounds in increasing order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n2 &\\ge-2\\sin\\theta\\ge-2 \\\\\n-1 &\\ge-2\\sin\\theta-3\\ge-5 \\\\\n-5 &\\le x\\le -1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-5\\le x\\le -1$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\cos\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiply by $2$ and translate by $-2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n-2 &\\le 2\\cos\\theta\\le 2 \\\\\n-4 &\\le 2\\cos\\theta-2\\le 0 \\\\\n-4 &\\le y\\le 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-4\\le y\\le 0$.\n\nContinuity gives every value between the stated bounds. The coordinate extrema need not occur at the same parameter value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698254 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nx=-3\\sin\\theta-3,\\qquad y=-4\\cos\\theta+3,\\\\\n0\\le \\theta<2\\pi,\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}} {{field-2}} $x\\le$ {{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}} $\\le y$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Consider each coordinate separately.\n\nFor $x$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\sin\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiplying by $-3$ reverses both comparisons; translate by $-3$, then write the bounds in increasing order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\sin\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n3 &\\ge-3\\sin\\theta\\ge-3 \\\\\n0 &\\ge-3\\sin\\theta-3\\ge-6 \\\\\n-6 &\\le x\\le 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-6\\le x\\le 0$.\n\nFor $y$:\n\nOver the full turn, $\\cos\\theta$ attains both $-1$ and $1$, including at permitted parameter values.\n\nMultiplying by $-4$ reverses both comparisons; translate by $3$, then write the bounds in increasing order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &\\le \\cos\\theta\\le 1 \\\\\n4 &\\ge-4\\cos\\theta\\ge-4 \\\\\n7 &\\ge-4\\cos\\theta+3\\ge-1 \\\\\n-1 &\\le y\\le 7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $-1\\le y\\le 7$.\n\nContinuity gives every value between the stated bounds. The coordinate extrema need not occur at the same parameter value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698382 :question/problem "Let $a=k+3i-2j$ and $b=2j+4k-i$. Use a determinant to find $a\\times b$.\n\nEnter each signed coefficient, including any minus sign.\n\n$a\\times b=$ {{field-1}} $i\\,+\\,$ {{field-2}} $j\\,+\\,$ {{field-3}} $k$" :question/worked-solution "First collect the coefficients in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\\langle 3,-2,1\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle -1,2,4\\rangle$. The order in which the terms are written does not change their coordinate positions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na\\times b\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\3&-2&1\\\\-1&2&4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2&1\\\\2&4\\end{vmatrix}i\n-\\begin{vmatrix}3&1\\\\-1&4\\end{vmatrix}j\n+\\begin{vmatrix}3&-2\\\\-1&2\\end{vmatrix}k\\\\\n&=((-2)(4)-(1)(2))i\\\\\n&\\quad-((3)(4)-(1)(-1))j\\\\\n&\\quad+((3)(2)-(-2)(-1))k\\\\\n&=(-8-2)i-(12+1)j+(6-2)k\\\\\n&=-10i-13j+4k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe three entries are $-10$, $-13$, and $4$. The plus signs printed between the fields combine signed coefficients; they do not make a negative coefficient positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698391 :question/problem "Let $a=2k-i+3j$ and $b=-2j+4i+k$. A student tries to calculate $a\\times b$ by writing\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\4&-2&1\\\\-1&3&2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nand then expanding along the top row using the cofactor signs $+,+,+$.\n\nWhich correction makes the setup and cofactor signs correct for $a\\times b$?\n\n{{correction}}\n\nNow calculate $a\\times b$ using the corrected determinant. Enter each signed coefficient.\n\n$a\\times b=$ {{field-1}} $i\\,+\\,$ {{field-2}} $j\\,+\\,$ {{field-3}} $k$" :question/worked-solution "Read the coordinates in $i,j,k$ order: $a=\\langle -1,3,2\\rangle$ and $b=\\langle 4,-2,1\\rangle$. For $a\\times b$, the second row must contain $a$ and the third row must contain $b$. The student's rows are reversed. Independently, the top-row cofactor signs must be $+,-,+$, not $+,+,+$.\n\nThus both corrections are needed: swap the vector rows and change the middle cofactor sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na\\times b\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\-1&3&2\\\\4&-2&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&2\\\\-2&1\\end{vmatrix}i\n-\\begin{vmatrix}-1&2\\\\4&1\\end{vmatrix}j\n+\\begin{vmatrix}-1&3\\\\4&-2\\end{vmatrix}k\\\\\n&=((3)(1)-(2)(-2))i\\\\\n&\\quad-((-1)(1)-(2)(4))j\\\\\n&\\quad+((-1)(-2)-(3)(4))k\\\\\n&=(3+4)i-(-1-8)j+(2-12)k\\\\\n&=7i+9j-10k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwapping the rows alone leaves a middle-sign error. Fixing the cofactor signs alone calculates $b\\times a$ instead. The signed entries for the requested product are $7$, $9$, and $-10$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698399 :question/problem "Let $u=\\langle 2,-1,0\\rangle$ and $v=\\langle 1,3,2\\rangle$. Use a determinant to find $u\\times v$." :question/worked-solution "Put $u$ in the second row and $v$ in the third row. Expand along the first row with signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu\\times v\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\2&-1&0\\\\1&3&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-1&0\\\\3&2\\end{vmatrix}i\n-\\begin{vmatrix}2&0\\\\1&2\\end{vmatrix}j\n+\\begin{vmatrix}2&-1\\\\1&3\\end{vmatrix}k\\\\\n&=((-1)(2)-(0)(3))i\\\\\n&\\quad-((2)(2)-(0)(1))j\\\\\n&\\quad+((2)(3)-(-1)(1))k\\\\\n&=-2i-4j+7k\\\\\n&=\\langle -2,-4,7\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe middle minor is $4$, but the middle component is $-4$ because its cofactor carries a minus sign."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698403 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=3 - 4 t\\\\ y&=t - 1\\\\ &-3\\le t\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First calculate the $x$-values using $x=3 - 4 t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & -3 & -2 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\hline x & 15 & 11 & 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & 2 & 3 \\\\ \\hline x & -5 & -9 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow calculate $y=t - 1$ at the **same parameter values**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & -3 & -2 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\hline x & 15 & 11 & 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ \\hline y & -4 & -3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & 2 & 3 \\\\ \\hline x & -5 & -9 \\\\ \\hline y & 1 & 2 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nPair the coordinates at the same parameter value and follow the curve only over the stated interval.\n\n![Curve through the calculated points](images/f8/f87af4c6b109422b771adb8747bd3e9eedb2c36bf77604ecd6b73c3fc50a8a1c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698404 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 \\sqrt{t} - 1\\\\ y&=t + 1\\\\ &0\\le t\\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First calculate the $x$-values using $x=2 \\sqrt{t} - 1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ \\hline x & -1 & 1 & -1 + 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\hline x & -1 + 2 \\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow calculate $y=t + 1$ at the **same parameter values**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ \\hline x & -1 & 1 & -1 + 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\hline y & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\hline x & -1 + 2 \\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ \\hline y & 4 & 5 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nPair the coordinates at the same parameter value and follow the curve only over the stated interval.\n\n![Curve through the calculated points](images/11/110abee7113f372d21760b21b3d5bb937ac30d16cba61b8a143b4a00136d1ed7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698408 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\\\ y&=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\\\ &0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First calculate the $x$-values using $x=2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{\\theta} & 0 & \\frac{\\pi}{6} & \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\\\ \\hline x & 3 & 1 + \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{\\theta} & \\frac{2 \\pi}{3} & \\pi & \\frac{4 \\pi}{3} \\\\ \\hline x & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{\\theta} & \\frac{5 \\pi}{3} \\\\ \\hline x & 2 \\\\ \\hline y &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow calculate $y=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1$ at the **same parameter values**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{\\theta} & 0 & \\frac{\\pi}{6} & \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\\\ \\hline x & 3 & 1 + \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ \\hline y & 1 & 3 & 1 + 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{\\theta} & \\frac{2 \\pi}{3} & \\pi & \\frac{4 \\pi}{3} \\\\ \\hline x & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\hline y & 1 + 2 \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 1 - 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{\\theta} & \\frac{5 \\pi}{3} \\\\ \\hline x & 2 \\\\ \\hline y & 1 - 2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nPair the coordinates at the same parameter value and follow the curve only over the stated interval.\n\n![Curve through the calculated points](images/42/4257b66e25b49edbc656a9f008b54eb60c3dd811bf666307cf3b7bf20c74d04e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698430 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 t - 1\\\\ y&=-1 + \\frac{5}{t}\\\\ &t>0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First calculate the $x$-values using $x=2 t - 1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\hline x & 0 & 1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & 5 \\\\ \\hline x & 9 \\\\ \\hline y &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow calculate $y=-1 + \\frac{5}{t}$ at the **same parameter values**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\hline x & 0 & 1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ \\hline y & 9 & 4 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & 5 \\\\ \\hline x & 9 \\\\ \\hline y & 0 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nPair the coordinates at the same parameter value and follow the curve only over the stated interval.\n\n![Curve through the calculated points](images/f8/f8c89c1d92cd8256446fdf43bce595b02637ba2ed74cebffea6044f8f394c61b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698431 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=5 - 2 t^{2}\\\\ y&=2 t + 2\\\\ &-1\\le t\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First calculate the $x$-values using $x=5 - 2 t^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & -1 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ \\hline x & 3 & 5 & 3 & -3 & -13 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow calculate $y=2 t + 2$ at the **same parameter values**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline \\mathbf{t} & -1 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ \\hline x & 3 & 5 & 3 & -3 & -13 \\\\ \\hline y & 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 & 8 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nPair the coordinates at the same parameter value and follow the curve only over the stated interval.\n\n![Curve through the calculated points](images/e0/e06b71f5c9e0f3076a8dcd534fa24ce5b319a7568e70b63f857e6150537e4ec7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698488 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle1,2,x\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle2,-1,x\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=\\sqrt{55}$, find all real values of $x$." :question/worked-solution "First compute the cross product using the determinant formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&2&x\\\\2&-1&x\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2&x\\\\-1&x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf i-\\begin{vmatrix}1&x\\\\2&x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf j+\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\2&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=(2x+x)\\mathbf i-(x-2x)\\mathbf j+(-1-4)\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=\\langle3x,x,-5\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute its magnitude and solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|&=\\sqrt{(3x)^2+x^2+(-5)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{10x^2+25},\\\\\n\\sqrt{10x^2+25}&=\\sqrt{55}\\\\\n10x^2+25&=55\\\\\nx^2&=3\\\\\nx&=\\pm\\sqrt3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor either $x=-\\sqrt3$ or $x=\\sqrt3$, the squared magnitude is $10(3)+25=55$, so both satisfy the original equation. The complete solution set is $x=\\pm\\sqrt3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698489 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf a=\\langle x,1,2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle2,0,3\\rangle$. Given that $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=\\sqrt{38}$, find all real values of $x$.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values are $x=$ {{field-1}} and $x=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\x&1&2\\\\2&0&3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\0&3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf i-\\begin{vmatrix}x&2\\\\2&3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf j+\\begin{vmatrix}x&1\\\\2&0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=(3-0)\\mathbf i-(3x-4)\\mathbf j+(0-2)\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=\\langle3,4-3x,-2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts magnitude gives a quadratic equation with a shifted square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3^2+(4-3x)^2+(-2)^2}&=\\sqrt{38}\\\\\n13+(4-3x)^2&=38\\\\\n(4-3x)^2&=25\\\\\n4-3x&=5\\quad\\text{or}\\quad4-3x=-5\\\\\nx&=-\\frac13\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, expanding and factoring gives $9x^2-24x-9=3(3x+1)(x-3)=0$. For $x=-\\frac13$, the cross product is $\\langle3,5,-2\\rangle$; for $x=3$, it is $\\langle3,-5,-2\\rangle$. Each has magnitude $\\sqrt{9+25+4}=\\sqrt{38}$.\n\nThus, in ascending order, the values are $-\\frac13$ and $3$."}]}
